From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 18:31:21 +0300
Subject: drawlib: GL state set only when drawing, DrawLib's primitives batched
 (XMOTO_GL_BATCH)

On the PS3 (Mesa nv30, one in-order PPE core) the credits replay spends most of its CPU in
Mesa and the kernel per GL call and per draw: about 18500 GL calls and 1960 draws a frame,
1009 glBegin/glEnd pairs among them (933 of them the minimap's polygons) and 954
glDrawArrays of one polygon each, every sprite switching GL_BLEND and GL_TEXTURE_2D off and
on again.

xm_OpenGL.h now routes every GL call of the game through a small layer (drawlib/GLBatch).
GL_TEXTURE_2D, GL_BLEND, GL_ALPHA_TEST, the bound texture, the blend and alpha functions,
the current colour, the client arrays and the array buffer are recorded and reach the GL
only when something is drawn, and only where they differ. DrawLib's startDraw/glVertex/
endDraw and the font code no longer use glBegin/glEnd: their polygons and quads go as
triangles into one client vertex array (position, texcoord, colour), drawn with one
glDrawArrays per run of equal state. Any other GL call first draws what is pending and sets
the recorded state, so every draw sees the state the game's own calls gave it, in the same
order; state changes between startDraw and endDraw are ignored as the GL ignored them inside
glBegin/glEnd. The triangles follow the order Mesa splits GL_POLYGON and GL_QUADS into, so
llvmpipe draws the same pixels. The ghost overlay's fade quad gave its vertices to the GL
directly; it goes through DrawLib now.

XMOTO_GL_BATCH=0 turns it off (glBegin/glEnd as before); XMOTO_GL_STATS=1 prints draws,
vertices and syncs per frame every 5 s (stderr and xmoto.log). The layer also draws ranges of
a static buffer (drawStatic, for the level buffer of the next patch, XMOTO_GL_BLOCKS).

